lock;width:100%;height:100%}.curve{height:175px;margin-top:10px}.metric-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:8px;margin:12px 0}.metric{padding:9px;background:#f5f7f2;border-radius:7px}.metric span{display:block;font-size:11px;color:var(--muted)}.metric strong{font-size:20px;font-weight:550}.features{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:6px;font-size:12px;margin-top:12px}.feature{border-bottom:1px solid #e4eae3;padding:5px}.feature strong{float:right}.legend{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted);display:flex;gap:14px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:7px}.legend i{display:inline-block;width:15px;border-top:3px solid;vertical-align:middle;margin-right:5px}details{margin-top:11px}summary{cursor:pointer;color:var(--ink);font-size:14px}details p{font-size:13px;color:var(--muted)}.links{display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:16px;font-size:13px}button:focus-visible,select:focus-visible,a:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #d3ac4f;outline-offset:2px}footer{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted);margin:18px 0}.pending{color:var(--muted);padding:12px 0}@media(max-width:800px){main{padding:16px}header{display:block}h1{font-size:25px}header a{display:inline-block;margin-top:12px}.columns{grid-template-columns:1fr}.counts{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}.card{padding:14px}.metric-row{gap:5px}.metric strong{font-size:17px}.diagram{height:265px}th,td{padding:8px}.features{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
</style></head><body><main>
<header><h1>Learning local settlement responses</h1><a class="button" href="transfer.html">Replay the coupled constructions →</a></header>
<p class="intro">A context table, a small regression and a small neural function learn one local decision: how much to limit competing requests at a port. Each predicts the usefulness of the same six possible bounds. Their exported programs run through one Gojo reader.</p>
<div id="counts" class="counts"></div>
<section class="card"><h2>Unseen local problems</h2><p class="small-note">Lower missed relief is better. It measures the benefit missed compared with the best of six available responses under the scalar teaching law, in percentage points of starting disagreement. These scores are not recognition accuracy or human probabilities.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Response policy</th><th>Fitted values</th><th>Mean missed relief</th><th>Mean relief</th><th>Wider requests: missed relief</th></tr></thead><tbody id="metrics"></tbody></table></div><p id="finding" class="small-note"></p></section>
<div class="columns"><section class="card"><h2>Inspect an unseen local decision</h2><p class="small-note">Each dot is a requested displacement of one receiver. The teacher applies a quarter response, an eight-tick step limit and a whole-tick quantum, then measures the remaining local disagreement.</p>
<div class="toolbar"><button id="prev" aria-label="Previous local problem">←</button><select id="example" aria-label="Local problem"></select><button id="next" aria-label="Next local problem">→</button></div><div id="methods" class="methods"></div><div id="diagram" class="diagram"></div><div id="decisionMetrics" class="metric-row"></div><div id="features" class="features"></div><p class="small-note">← / → problem · ↑ / ↓ policy. The request plot and the zoomed response use different declared scales.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Alternatives at this port</h2><p class="small-note">Model columns predict relief on the teaching task. The last column is the measured finite teacher read. Green marks a best available response; the selected policy's choice is shaded.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Bound</th><th>Table</th><th>Regression</th><th>Neural</th><th>Measured</th></tr></thead><tbody id="alternatives"></tbody></table></div><p id="selection" class="small-note"></p><details><summary>What the model receives and predicts</summary><p>Its inputs are six coarse, reflection-invariant reads of the public request group. It receives no category, desired final coordinates, private actuator state or final drawing. Its six outputs estimate relative one-step relief for the six bounded responses. The priority for equal scores keeps the existing twelve-tick choice.</p><p>The teacher is authored scalar arithmetic. It does not label an ideal church. The coupled constructions below are a separate transfer task with shared actuators, multiple obligations and structural changes.</p></details></section></div>
<section class="card"><h2>Transfer to connected constructions</h2><p class="small-note">The fitted functions are frozen before these runs. The fixed local negotiation is the matched baseline: it has the same response mixture, receiver capacities, joint review and support checks. Lower final known disagreement is better under this fixture's diagnostic.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Cooperative: final</th><th>Cooperative: during run</th><th>Conflicting: final</th><th>Conflicting: during run</th></tr></thead><tbody id="transfer"></tbody></table></div><p id="transferNote" class="small-note"></p><div class="links"><a href="transfer.html">Replay every visual transfer case</a><a href="observations.json" download>Transfer observations</a></div></section>
<section id="coverageCard" class="card" hidden><h2>A separate count-coverage probe</h2><p class="small-note">After inspecting transfer failures, this two-case probe restricts the same neural function to request counts actually observed in training. An unavailable model read keeps the fixed twelve-tick response as a separate fallback. No coefficients are retrained. These inspected cases do not establish held-out performance.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Inspected input</th><th>Fixed: final</th><th>Raw neural: final</th><th>Count-qualified neural: final</th><th>Raw contexts outside training counts</th></tr></thead><tbody id="coverage"></tbody></table></div><p id="coverageNote" class="small-note"></p><a href="coverage.html">Inspect the count-qualified replay →</a></section>
<div class="columns"><section class="card"><h2>Learning and model selection</h2><p class="small-note">The neural fit has six inputs, eight bounded positive hidden nodes and six outputs: 110 fitted values. Three initializations use the same training set. Checkpoints use validation prediction error; the exported initialization is selected by validation missed relief.</p><div id="curve" class="curve"></div><div id="seedLegend" class="legend"></div><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Initialization</th><th>Kept epoch</th><th>Validation MSE</th><th>Validation missed relief</th></tr></thead><tbody id="seeds"></tbody></table></div></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Scope and retained structure</h2><p class="small-note">Whole request multisets and their reflections are kept out of other splits. The full run also excludes the development pilot and uses fresh seeds. Wider-request problems double the generator's amplitude range.</p><p class="small-note">All three forms retain their domains, affine frames, output bounds, quantization and training source. Inference preserves clipping and rounding losses. Native restoration is checked against the original program. Numerical fitting runs offline in C#; this does not implement gradient learning through Gojo tension.</p><details><summary>Fit and export measurements</summary><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Fit and check seconds</th><th>Sampled export difference</th></tr></thead><tbody id="exports"></tbody></table></div><p>Export difference compares the fitted floating function with the finite native program on 32 validation problems. It is a sampled diagnostic, not a guaranteed error bound. Table values have a declared finite import, with no separate floating evaluator in this measurement.</p></details><div id="archives" class="links"></div><div class="links"><a href="learning.json" download>Learning results and examples</a></div></section></div>
<footer>Authoring observations, local inference, structural review and numerical admission remain separate. Good local predictions can still give a poor coupled outcome.</footer>
